+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"agent_message","text":"All 34 tests pass, `npm run build` and `npm run lint` are clean. Here are the 6 bugs I fixed:\n\n**`src/events/bus.ts`** — Handlers were stored in a module-level `globalHandlers` map shared across all `EventBus` instances. This caused handlers to leak between tests/services, so stale handlers from prior test setups fired on later tests, producing the cascading \"Insufficient stock\" errors. Fixed by making `handlers` an instance field.\n\n**`src/orders/repository.ts:38`** — `update()` built the new order from `{ ...updates, id, updatedAt }` instead of merging with `existing`, so any field not in `updates` was lost. Fixed by spreading `existing` first: `{ ...existing, ...updates, id, updatedAt }`.\n\n**`src/billing/service.ts:56`** — `handleRefund` was registered without `.bind(this)`, so `this.payments` was `undefined` when the handler ran, crashing the refund flow. Fixed by binding it (matching `handleStockReserved`).\n\n**`src/billing/calculator.ts`** — `afterDiscount` was rounded to 2 decimals before computing tax, causing double-rounding (`130.8626` → `130.87` instead of `130.86`). Fixed by rounding only the final `total`, keeping intermediate values at full precision.\n\n**`src/orders/service.ts:99`** — Refund used `>= REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS`, rejecting refunds on exactly day 30. Fixed to `> REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS` so day 30 is allowed and day 31 is rejected.\n\n**`src/inventory/service.ts`** — `handleOrderCancelled` required `event.status === 'cancelled'`, but `OrderService.cancelOrder` doesn't set a `status` field on the cancellation event, so stock was never released on real cancellations. Removed the fragile guard so it releases stock based on the `items` array."}}
